Oh, I see - the PDI debug output was truncated (I only saw the tail). Let me look at the full PDI output.

Actually, the issue is that valid_isolate_count = 124 and iso_level = 124. So they're equal. When PDIs come, both decrement together. After 124 PDIs, both = 0. No underflow.

So I need valid_isolate_count > isolate_level. Since isolate_level caps at 124, I need valid_isolate_count > 124, i.e., 125+ valid isolates.

But the 125th isolate gave valid=124. Why? Because the 125th isolate's new_level = 125, which is valid, so valid_isolate_count should become 125. But the debug shows valid=124.

Wait, let me recount. The debug shows the state BEFORE the increment. Let me re-read the code:

```c
RL_LEVEL (pp) = level;
RL_ISOLATE_LEVEL (pp) = isolate_level;
if (isolate_level < FRIBIDI_BIDI_MAX_EXPLICIT_LEVEL-1)
    isolate_level++;
...
if (new_level <= FRIBIDI_BIDI_MAX_EXPLICIT_LEVEL)
{
    valid_isolate_count++;
    PUSH_STATUS;
    level = new_level;
}
```

So the debug print is placed before the `if (new_level <= ...)` check. So it shows the state before valid_isolate_count++.

For the last push: "ISO push: level=124 new_level=125 iso_level=124 valid=124". This means before this push, valid=124. After the push (valid++), valid=125. And iso_level stays 124 (capped).

